<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ebroin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 681</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ebroin was mayor of the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>, the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a>, from about 658 until his murder around 681. Ruling in the names of Merovingian kings, he fought the aristocracies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a> and had his opponent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leodegar">Leodegar of Autun</a> blinded and later executed. He defeated the Austrasians, but after his death they gained the upper hand under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pippin-of-herstal">Pippin of Herstal</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ebroin.jpg" alt="Ebroin" width="297" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Atelier de Paris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denier_d%27Ebro%C3%AFn_au_nom_du_mon%C3%A9taire_Rodemarus_frapp%C3%A9_%C3%A0_Paris.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ebroin was mayor of the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> on two occasions, first from 658 until his deposition in 673 and again from 675 until his death. After the failed coup of the Austrasian mayor Grimoald the Elder, the Merovingian court resided in Neustria. According to the Liber historiae Francorum, a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> elected Ebroin in 659 to replace Erchinoald, who had died in the reign of Chlothar III. Queen Balthild served as regent for her son Chlothar III and, after a power struggle with Ebroin, withdrew to the Abbey of Chelles in 664.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> records that in 668 Ebroin waylaid an Englishman returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, for fear that the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> was plotting an alliance against him. When Chlothar III died in 673, Ebroin raised another brother, Theuderic III, to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> without consulting the nobles. He tried to keep Neustria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> united, but the Burgundian nobles, led by Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leodegar">Leodegar of Autun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalrich-duke-of-alsace">Adalrich, Duke of Alsace</a>, offered the crown to Childeric II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>. Childeric replaced Ebroin with his own mayor, Wulfoald. Ebroin was tonsured and confined to Luxeuil. A proclamation then declared that each kingdom would keep its own laws and that no one should again set up a tyranny like Ebroin's.</p><p>Childeric II was murdered at Bondi, and Ebroin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leodegar">Leodegar</a> left their cloister. Ebroin proclaimed a false Merovingian as king, had the mayor Leudesius murdered and made himself mayor again. About 675 he reimposed his authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> and most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a>, where Leodegar's eyes were put out. Ebroin persuaded the king that Childeric's murder had been Leodegar's doing, and Leodegar was tried and exiled. On 12 October 678 he was led away and murdered. Ebroin also defeated the Austrasians at the Battle of Bois-du-Fays, near Laon. Duke Martin, lured to Ecry with false assurances of talks with Theuderic, was killed there with his supporters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ebroin was assassinated in a combined attack by his many enemies. In 684 Ansoald, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, commissioned a Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leodegar">Leodegar</a> that presented Ebroin as an enemy of God driven only by ambition and a lust for power. It became so well established in the Church that Leodegar was canonised as Saint Leger. The Austrasians, whose own ambitious mayoral family commissioned the continuations of the chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a>, also found tales of Ebroin's infamy useful.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ebroin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Neustria had a strongly Gallo-Roman population and rich abbeys such as Saint-Denis, and in the 7th century its mayor <b>Ebroin</b> dominated politics.</blockquote><a href="/state/neustria">Neustria</a></li></ul></section>
